---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
import MarketTimingLab from "../../components/MarketTimingLab.tsx";
import NextTool from "../../components/NextTool.astro";
import Sources from "../../components/Sources.astro";
import "../../styles/simtool.css";
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Time in the Market"
  description="What does missing the market's best days cost you? Using decades of daily US market returns, watch a handful of missed days turn a healthy return into almost nothing — because the best days hide right next to the worst."
>
  <section class="container wl-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">Interactive Tool</p>
    <h1>Time in the market</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      When markets get scary, the tempting move is to sell and wait for calm. The
      problem: the market's very best days tend to arrive right in the middle of
      the fear — often within days of the worst ones. Miss a handful of them and
      decades of growth can evaporate. Switch between two views: what <strong>missing
      the best days</strong> costs a lump sum, and how even the world's <strong>worst
      market timer</strong> — someone who only ever buys at the peak before a crash —
      still runs circles around the saver who waits in cash.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-host">
    <MarketTimingLab client:load />
  </section>

  <section class="container wl-explainer">
    <div class="prose">
      <h2>Why timing is so dangerous</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>The best days hide next to the worst.</strong> Big up-days and
          big down-days cluster together, because sharp rebounds tend to follow
          sharp drops. To sell before the fall and buy back before the recovery, you'd
          have to be right twice, within days — and almost no one is.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>A few days carry the whole return.</strong> Long-run returns
          aren't spread evenly across time; a tiny number of days do most of the
          work. Being out of the market for even a few of them takes a wrecking ball
          to the result — which is exactly what the chart shows.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Staying put is the "boring" edge.</strong> The investor who does
          nothing during a panic captures those rebound days automatically. That's
          the whole case for staying invested through the scary parts: you can't catch
          the best days if you're not there for them.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        This isn't an argument that markets never fall — they do, sometimes brutally.
        It's that reacting to the falls by getting out tends to cost more than the
        falls themselves. Educational only, not advice.
      </p>

      <h2>The world's worst market timer</h2>
      <p>
        Flip the tool to <strong>"Timing your buys."</strong> Four people save the
        same amount every month for decades. One has terrible luck (or terrible
        instincts) and only ever moves his cash into stocks at the market's peak —
        right before each crash. Another nails every bottom. A third just invests each
        paycheck as it lands. The fourth, spooked, keeps it all in cash.
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <strong>Even the worst timer wins big.</strong> Buying only at the peaks
          sounds catastrophic, but he never sold — so every dollar still rode the
          recovery to the end. Decades of compounding from a bad starting price still
          beats no compounding at all.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Cash is the real loser.</strong> The saver who stayed out to avoid
          the drops ended up with barely more than he put in, once inflation is
          counted. Avoiding every crash also means avoiding every recovery.
        </li>
        <li>
          <strong>Perfect timing barely beats autopilot.</strong> Nailing every bottom
          only about ties investing steadily — because the cash you hold while waiting
          for the dip gives back most of what the dip would have saved you.
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        The lesson isn't that timing is easy or that crashes don't hurt. It's that
        <em>being in the market</em> does the heavy lifting, and the cost of sitting
        out dwarfs the cost of buying at the wrong time. Educational only, not advice.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <Sources tool="time-in-market" />

  <NextTool current="/tools/time-in-market" />
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .wl-intro {
    padding-block: var(--space-lg) var(--space-md);
    max-width: var(--measure);
  }
  .lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
    line-height: 1.5;
  }
  .wl-explainer {
    margin-top: var(--space-lg);
  }
</style>

<style is:global>
  .ss-headline {
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    gap: 0.15rem;
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    background: var(--color-accent-soft);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-accent);
  }
  .ss-headline-label {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
  }
  .ss-headline-value {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--step-3);
    line-height: 1.1;
    color: var(--color-accent);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
  .ss-headline-sub {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-text-soft);
  }
  .ss-stats {
    display: grid;
    grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr;
    gap: var(--space-sm);
    margin: 0 0 var(--space-md);
  }
  .ss-stats > div {
    background: var(--color-surface-alt);
    border-radius: var(--radius);
    padding: var(--space-sm);
  }
  .ss-stats dt {
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-size: var(--step--1);
    color: var(--color-muted);
    margin-bottom: 0.2rem;
  }
  .ss-stats dd {
    margin: 0;
    font-family: var(--font-sans);
    font-weight: 700;
    font-size: var(--step-0);
    font-variant-numeric: tabular-nums;
  }
</style>
